00:14:52.460 --> 00:14:53.500
marc | wolovim: Yeah, no, I can't.

84
00:14:55.100 --> 00:14:56.450
raxhvl: Yeah, give me… give me a sec.

85
00:15:04.520 --> 00:15:06.099
marc | wolovim: There we go, we got full screen now.

86
00:15:31.980 --> 00:15:36.820
raxhvl: Okay, so I want to talk about Xi frames.

87
00:15:37.350 --> 00:15:40.390
raxhvl: These are frames that,

88
00:15:40.780 --> 00:15:46.369
raxhvl: You know, ensure that a transaction does not get executed after a point in time.

89
00:15:46.970 --> 00:15:53.730
raxhvl: Briefly, how this works is you have a special frame which has no flags, values, or…

90
00:15:53.950 --> 00:15:55.030
raxhvl: Cast limit?

91
00:15:55.490 --> 00:16:01.060
raxhvl: And it targets an EVM contract, which, checks for a timestamp.

92
00:16:01.230 --> 00:16:04.830
raxhvl: And, if it's expired, you rewards.

93
00:16:04.970 --> 00:16:08.940
raxhvl: Now, the contract, which, fails the frame.

94
00:16:09.190 --> 00:16:12.429
raxhvl: And then, the transaction is mapped invalid.

95
00:16:13.120 --> 00:16:15.310
raxhvl: This has some moving parts.

96
00:16:15.590 --> 00:16:20.559
raxhvl: But, the intent, of what we're trying to do is a simple check.

97
00:16:20.710 --> 00:16:25.940
raxhvl: Whether, the block timestamp is… is greater than the expiry.

98
00:16:26.440 --> 00:16:32.739
raxhvl: Also, we note that the expiry applies to not an individual frame, but the transaction as a whole.

99
00:16:33.130 --> 00:16:42.319
raxhvl: And so… As opposed to a frame, this information fits naturally into the transaction metadata.

100
00:16:42.960 --> 00:16:48.140
raxhvl: And if you do that, it kind of simplifies, some things.

101
00:16:48.610 --> 00:16:51.929
raxhvl: First of which is, state.

102
00:16:52.460 --> 00:16:57.289
raxhvl: Expiry validation is a pure function which takes the current block and transaction.

103
00:16:58.050 --> 00:17:02.500
raxhvl: And, returns a Boolean value, if the transaction is valid or not.

104
00:17:03.070 --> 00:17:08.989
raxhvl: Bringing stateless, to this equation complicates, quite a lot of things, like tooling.

105
00:17:09.390 --> 00:17:13.339
raxhvl: Mempool validation, efficiency, and network parity.

106
00:17:13.859 --> 00:17:15.300
raxhvl: And this is because

107
00:17:15.460 --> 00:17:26.949
raxhvl: Now, the pure computation has to flow through state. Of course, we can have a native implementation that bypasses EVM, but we still gotta check if the contact is actually present in state.

108
00:17:27.250 --> 00:17:32.620
raxhvl: And, if not, there's a drift, and all this takes a hit on performance.

109
00:17:34.530 --> 00:17:42.660
raxhvl: This is caveated in the IP text, in two fronts. First is that, obviously, we need a native implementation for performance.

110
00:17:43.100 --> 00:17:52.080
raxhvl: Second, there is no behavior guarantee across networks, right? It could be that a certain network has not

111
00:17:52.560 --> 00:17:57.640
raxhvl: Deployed a contract, and all this is tied to the current state of network.

112
00:17:59.520 --> 00:18:02.960
raxhvl: It has impact on tooling, this is,

113
00:18:03.790 --> 00:18:07.960
raxhvl: a sample PR that has… that is just spoken about.

114
00:18:08.300 --> 00:18:15.490
raxhvl: This installs the expiry, verify a contract into the testing framework.

115
00:18:16.300 --> 00:18:20.399
raxhvl: This has about 200 lines of, code.

116
00:18:21.540 --> 00:18:26.769
raxhvl: For what we discussed, right? It's just a simple line check, and this feels like…

117
00:18:27.580 --> 00:18:32.319
raxhvl: A bit disproportional, given the intent is to do a simple check.

118
00:18:33.790 --> 00:18:39.550
raxhvl: If we add expiry to the payload, of the transaction itself, we can…

119
00:18:39.960 --> 00:18:42.729
raxhvl: Get some guarantees, by design.

120
00:18:43.300 --> 00:18:47.439
raxhvl: First is that we don't need to check if we have multiplex value frames.

121
00:18:47.590 --> 00:18:56.429
raxhvl: Because the payload has only one position for expiry, it guarantees that, you know, there can be only one expiry timestamp for a transaction.

122
00:18:56.770 --> 00:19:03.419
raxhvl: And because we get, rid of the special frame, we can get… Rid of its constraint.

123
00:19:03.620 --> 00:19:09.430
raxhvl: Such as checking whether the flags and, values are zero, and there are no state caps.

124
00:19:11.210 --> 00:19:14.989
raxhvl: There are also some exceptions we make for this particular frame.

125
00:19:15.640 --> 00:19:24.519
raxhvl: First is that, verify Ethereum generally don't, include their call data as part of the signature?

126
00:19:24.730 --> 00:19:30.420
raxhvl: But we gotta… add, the call data-flow expiry Frame into the signature, because

127
00:19:30.580 --> 00:19:35.369
raxhvl: That has the expiry information, and we don't want, it to be tampered.

128
00:19:35.930 --> 00:19:43.689
raxhvl: We also allow a band timestamp of code to be executed for, this special frame.

129
00:19:44.480 --> 00:19:48.200
raxhvl: If we… if we do a standard native expiry,

130
00:19:48.820 --> 00:19:55.350
raxhvl: The transaction payload is… contains the expiry and is automatically included in that signature.

131
00:19:55.610 --> 00:20:01.690
raxhvl: And also, we don't need to make any EVM exception, because we just bypassed EVM.

132
00:20:03.520 --> 00:20:07.770
raxhvl: It's… Also smaller.

133
00:20:08.140 --> 00:20:15.400
raxhvl: Because we're mostly encoding an empty frame. Towards the right, you can see that, you know, the flags are zero.

134
00:20:15.640 --> 00:20:23.410
raxhvl: We just have some execution gas, to do the pure competition, and the timestamp itself.

135
00:20:25.920 --> 00:20:35.440
raxhvl: So, if we just have a native integer, it's significantly smaller. That's one, it's about 9 bytes versus 41 bytes.

136
00:20:37.000 --> 00:20:43.210
raxhvl: Now, there is some trade-off, to adding this, to the payload.

137
00:20:44.070 --> 00:20:49.409
raxhvl: Expiry Frame allows you to define optional expiry.

138
00:20:49.840 --> 00:20:54.090
raxhvl: But if you add something to the payload, that becomes a mandatory field.

139
00:20:54.440 --> 00:20:57.849
raxhvl: Which adds a fixed 9-byte overhead.

140
00:20:58.310 --> 00:20:59.989
raxhvl: Across all transactions.

141
00:21:00.340 --> 00:21:04.159
raxhvl: And ideally, we should have the ability to define expiry as an option.

142
00:21:05.040 --> 00:21:14.290
raxhvl: That brings us to… The core of this proposal, it's, something called Transaction features.

143
00:21:15.090 --> 00:21:22.500
raxhvl: Features define, like, optional qualities of a transaction, these get applied To Transaction as a whole.

144
00:21:22.700 --> 00:21:28.799
raxhvl: And you can choose to define features that fits a specific transaction.

145
00:21:29.780 --> 00:21:34.239
raxhvl: Here's how that looks. Features are simple.

146
00:21:34.560 --> 00:21:43.149
raxhvl: Key value pairs. Keys are called feature tags. Values are arbitrary values that, you need for that feature.

147
00:21:45.340 --> 00:21:51.000
raxhvl: Feature tags, allows you to, you know, only define the features that you need.

148
00:21:51.580 --> 00:21:54.939
raxhvl: For example, if you need expiry, you can just have

149
00:21:55.340 --> 00:21:59.180
raxhvl: Have that tuple as part of your feature list.

150
00:21:59.380 --> 00:22:01.550
raxhvl: And if you don't, you can just remove it.

151
00:22:02.370 --> 00:22:08.980
raxhvl: And if a transaction has no feature at all, we just encode an empty… List?

152
00:22:09.100 --> 00:22:11.310
raxhvl: Which has very little overhead.

153
00:22:11.520 --> 00:22:16.270
raxhvl: Which, which is just a single byte RLP, header.

154
00:22:18.620 --> 00:22:23.790
raxhvl: features, need to follow canonical ordering. That means

155
00:22:24.210 --> 00:22:26.980
raxhvl: Each feature tag, which are just integers.

156
00:22:27.590 --> 00:22:32.869
raxhvl: Need to ensure that, you know, Follow a strictly ascending order.

157
00:22:33.620 --> 00:22:45.440
raxhvl: So if we follow that, we can ensure two properties. One is that no features can be duplicated in a transaction, so a transaction cannot have two expiry, for example.

158
00:22:46.490 --> 00:22:56.899
raxhvl: And, this gives you, like, deterministic, deterministic way to encode a transaction, which gives you, a canonical signature.

159
00:22:58.140 --> 00:23:03.460
raxhvl: On the price front, We can start with two features.

160
00:23:03.920 --> 00:23:11.450
raxhvl: There's a proposal that, that wants to add like,

161
00:23:11.760 --> 00:23:15.509
raxhvl: Valid from timestamps, so we start with a from and an until feature.

162
00:23:15.950 --> 00:23:21.850
raxhvl: These are simple comparisons, that have… negligible cost.

163
00:23:22.240 --> 00:23:29.080
raxhvl: On the value front, these are 8 byte each, and encoded as part of the transaction payload.

164
00:23:29.310 --> 00:23:32.299
raxhvl: This is quite similar to the call data.

165
00:23:32.600 --> 00:23:37.870
raxhvl: So we could, price it similarly, or, we can choose to not do that.

166
00:23:38.040 --> 00:23:39.629
raxhvl: We can have that.

167
00:23:39.860 --> 00:23:41.270
raxhvl: Up for discussion?

168
00:23:42.980 --> 00:23:51.149
raxhvl: And, yeah, that's about it. I have a DAS PR, and, yeah, that's all I've got.

169
00:23:52.100 --> 00:23:52.940
raxhvl: Thanks.

170
00:23:55.780 --> 00:23:56.630
marc | wolovim: Thanks, Rob.

171
00:23:56.870 --> 00:24:06.949
marc | wolovim: So, to clarify, this is a proposed change to Frame Transactions itself, or would be tackled in a, like, a separate EIP, for example?

172
00:24:07.880 --> 00:24:14.170
raxhvl: This is… This is not a native proposal to how features are implemented within FrameDirection.

173
00:24:16.130 --> 00:24:19.590
raxhvl: How expiring is implemented within FrameCon.

174
00:24:23.640 --> 00:24:28.620
marc | wolovim: Okay, I see Matt already left you a couple comments in the chat. Did anybody want to respond now?

175
00:24:42.080 --> 00:24:48.830
lightclient: Alright, maybe I can ask a more broad question. What is the motivation for…

176
00:24:49.560 --> 00:24:53.929
lightclient: You know, choosing to add this functionality in the protocol.

177
00:24:54.970 --> 00:25:00.870
lightclient: Because from my perspective, like, we can implement this outside of the protocol, and if you can implement something outside of the protocol.

178
00:25:01.300 --> 00:25:03.939
lightclient: Generally, you should implement it outside the protocol.

179
00:25:09.650 --> 00:25:14.730
raxhvl: The core motivation of that, the comparison is… stateless?

180
00:25:15.380 --> 00:25:21.080
raxhvl: And I was wondering why should we add state as a dependency, yeah?

181
00:25:21.300 --> 00:25:22.459
lightclient: What is the state?

182
00:25:24.140 --> 00:25:25.940
raxhvl: The EVM contract?

183
00:25:27.220 --> 00:25:29.150
lightclient: I mean, you don't have to…

184
00:25:29.310 --> 00:25:35.849
lightclient: Like, load this… this state, you can interpret it natively in the mempool.

185
00:25:37.430 --> 00:25:39.749
raxhvl: Yeah, but we still have to read the…

186
00:25:40.420 --> 00:25:44.140
raxhvl: State to ensure that the contract is deployed.

